    "q-20584": {
      "dom_id": "step-q20584",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Compute the value of $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$ for which the polar curve $r=2{e}^{\\theta }$ has a horizontal tangent, given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2{e}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2{e}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We have a horizontal tangent when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2{e}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2{e}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta $ where ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ \\theta  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only one belongs to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether our candidate has ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solution into ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and check that ${x}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.$ If ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =2{e}^{(3\\pi /4)}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{(3\\pi /4)} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, our curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227550/assets/267b0acf9101dd5fe999ab9b51e85cfee368c0d93667f596c86fb741fdf26aa5.png)",
