    "q-353034": {
      "dom_id": "step-q353034",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Compute the values of $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi )$ for which the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ has a horizontal tangent, given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn **ascending order**, the polar curve has horizontal tangents at\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We have a horizontal tangent when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta ).$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta $ where ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ 2\\theta  & =\\pi n \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only two belong to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=0,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and check that ${x}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.$ If ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is also a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our curve has horizontal tangents at ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227550/assets/b60ac3e170875d48a6bf3733122b79be16ddff27c0cb3878d8a3125d09bb3824.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
