    "q-20588": {
      "dom_id": "step-q20588",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Compute the value of $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ for which the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ has vertical tangents, given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We have a vertical tangent when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta $ where ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1) & =0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =0,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=0.\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1=0,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThere are no solutions to this equation for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether our candidate has ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solution into ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and check that ${y}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.$ If ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =-{sin}^{2}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+{cos}^{2}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ is a valid solution.\n\nSo, our curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227550/assets/acc9f079177aab7bf360a978cb398495f339c9ea243e06aa242c9c38f8028ca1.png)",
