    "q-363452": {
      "dom_id": "step-q363452",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Compute the value of $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for which the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ has a vertical tangent, given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nThe polar curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We have a vertical tangent when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta $ where ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 2\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi n \\\\ \\theta  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only one belongs to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether our candidate has ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solution into ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and check that ${y}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.$ If ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}))-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ is a valid solution.\n\nSo, our curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227550/assets/8c77297d85a8cd9a10ed219af49635fcf76412f80895a4af7a567f5ff9c49ce3.png)",
