    "q-363460": {
      "dom_id": "step-q363460",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Compute the values of $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$ for which the polar curve $r={e}^{\\theta /\\sqrt{3}}$ has vertical tangents, given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{\\theta /\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{\\theta /\\sqrt{3}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\n$$\n\nThe polar curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We have a vertical tangent when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{\\theta /\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{\\theta /\\sqrt{3}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta $ where ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\theta /\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only one belongs to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether our candidate has ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solution into ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and check that ${y}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.$ If ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={e}^{\\pi /(6\\sqrt{3})}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /(6\\sqrt{3})}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /(6\\sqrt{3})}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}{e}^{\\pi /(6\\sqrt{3})} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227550/assets/e6b79e2c733ca89760a962ab1ff619a116c18c5a0f014746a80a95ad95c939b5.png)",
